  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 AM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will maintain control of our weather over the \nnext several days. The dry airmass will lead to a larger diurnal \nrange with lows dropping into the 40s early this morning before \njumping into the upper 70s to near 80 this afternoon. Did adjust dew \npoints down a little bit from the NBM (similar to yesterday) which \nresults in minimum RH's falling into the 25-30% range in some areas. \nA slight increase in temperatures is expected through Thursday, but \nthen a wind shift back to northeasterly will usher in slightly \ncooler temperatures on Friday.\n\nWe continue to monitor a disturbance in the southwestern Gulf that \nis becoming increasingly likely (80% chance) to develop into a \ntropical system over the next 48 hours. Guidance suggests a possible \nlandfall occurring along the Gulf sometime on Saturday and then the \nmoisture streaming northward into the TN Valley Saturday night into \nSunday. There remains some spread amongst guidance on how far \nnorthwest to bring the rain shield though. West Kentucky and \nsouthwest Indiana seem to be the most favored regions to observe a \ndecent soaking rain at the moment. But this could easily shift east \nor west in the coming days.",
